# The Pledge - a pawnbroker, a christening mug with somebody else's name on it, and a redemption date.
# Written for the library. One person, one procedure, one decision: what to do with a thing you have noticed
# about a thing you are holding.

title: The Pledge
start: start

== start ==
bg lost_office
music room_tone
Three hundred and sixty days, and then it is yours to sell. That is the whole of
the trade and it fits on the ticket: the pledge, the sum, the rate, and the
date.
You have run the shop for twenty-two years. The window says GOLD - SILVER -
WATCHES and the rest of it is on the shelves behind you and has been for longer
than most of the people who bring things in.
The man at the counter is in his fifties and has put a box on the glass, which
is how you know it matters, because nobody puts a box down unless they have
decided to.
-> the_box

== the_box ==
It is a christening mug. Silver, probably, with the dents that come from being
used, and a band of worn engraving round the middle.
He watches you turn it over, which people do not usually.
you: What do you want for it?
man: Whatever you give.
you: That is not how it works. You tell me what you want and I tell you what I
can do.
man: Sixty.
The silver is worth ninety on a good day. You have been doing this long enough
to know that a man who asks for less than the thing is worth wants the ticket
and not the money, and that is a different transaction from the one in the
window.
-> the_engraving

== the_engraving ==
bg closet
You write the ticket out the way you write all of them: date, description, sum,
rate, and the date three hundred and sixty days from now, which you say out loud
because that is the part people need to hear.
man: Three hundred and sixty days.
you: The fourteenth. October the fourteenth.
You put the mug in the window box on the shelf behind you, on the second shelf,
which is where the things go that are worth keeping an eye on.
-> the_name

== the_name ==
You clean the engraving with your thumb, because you always do, and the band
reads *for Ellis, on his baptism*, and underneath, in smaller letters, a date
that makes the mug sixty years old.
The man's name is on the ticket. His name is not on the mug.
* Ask him whose it is. -> ask_him
* Say nothing. Write the ticket as it is. -> write_it
* Look at him for a moment longer than the trade needs. -> look_at_him

== ask_him ==
you: Ellis.
man: My brother.
you: And where is your brother?
man: Where do you think.
He says it without any weight at all, in the way people say a fact they have
carried a long way, and you have been in this trade long enough to know that the
next sentence will be a lie or the truth and that which one does not change the
silver.
man: It was going to be mine anyway. He'd have wanted -
He stops, because he can hear it.
man: I need the sixty.
~ asked +1
-> the_terms

== look_at_him ==
You look at him. He looks at the mug. Neither of you says anything for about
four seconds, which in a shop is a very long time.
man: It's not stolen, if that's what you're doing.
you: I'm not doing anything. I'm looking at a mug.
man: Sixty.
-> the_terms

== write_it ==
You write the ticket as it is: a christening mug, sixty pounds, three hundred and
sixty days. No name but his on the paperwork.
He takes his copy and folds it into his coat and goes out, and the bell over the
door does what it does, and the mug sits on the second shelf with a name on it
that is not on the ticket.
-> ending_quiet

== the_terms ==
You put his copy of the ticket on the glass and push it across, and he takes it
and does not read it, which is also how you know.
you: Three hundred and sixty days. After that the pledge is forfeit and the mug
is mine to sell. That is what the ticket says and it is what I will do.
man: I know what it says.
you: Do you want me to write your brother's name on my half?
if asked >= 1 -> the_answer
-> the_form

== the_answer ==
you: Do you want me to write your brother's name on my half?
He looks at the mug for a while, and then at the ticket, and then at you.
man: He was forty-one. It was in a drawer with his keys in it and I have the
keys.
you: That is not an answer to what I asked.
man: It's the answer you're going to get.
He takes his copy of the ticket and goes out, and the bell does what it does.
-> ending_named

== the_form ==
You put his copy on the glass and he takes it and folds it into his coat, and
neither of you says the name that is on the mug and not on the paper. The trade
is the trade. It has been for two hundred years and it is why anybody brings
anything in.
man: Three hundred and sixty days.
you: The fourteenth of October.
He goes out. The bell.
-> ending_forfeit

== ending_named ==
You write it on the shop's half in pencil, under the description, where the
auctioneers will find it in thirty years and wonder, and you put the mug back on
the second shelf and you do not sell it when the day comes.
He comes in on the eleventh month, which is not the fourteenth, with sixty
pounds and a bad apology, and you take the money and hand him the mug and do not
say anything about the pencil, because the pencil was for the mug and not for
him.
-> END

== ending_quiet ==
You put the mug back on the second shelf, and the ticket says what it says, and
the fourteenth of October comes round the way dates do.
He does not come. You did not expect him to and you are not sure whether that is
because you know the trade or because you looked at him for four seconds and
decided something, and you have been in this business twenty-two years and you
still cannot tell those two apart.
The mug is forfeit. It is yours to sell. It sits on the second shelf.
-> END

== ending_forfeit ==
The fourteenth of October comes and goes, and the mug is yours to sell, and in
November you put it in the window with a card: SILVER CHRISTENING MUG, 1966,
ENGRAVED, £140.
It sells in nine days to a woman buying it for a grandchild who is not called
Ellis, and she asks whether you can take the engraving off, and you say no,
because you cannot, and because it is the only part of it that was ever
anybody's.
-> END
